.TH KEYBOARD 6
.SH NAME
keyboard \- what keys deliver: bytes, runes, and the kbd file
.SH DESCRIPTION
A key that has a character types it.
Backspace, tab, escape and control characters are typed the ordinary
way: control-J is a line feed and control-M a carriage return.
The key labeled
.B Enter
or
.B Return
generates a newline
.RB ( 0x0A ).
.PP
A key with no character, an arrow, a function key, or a modifier by
itself, produces no byte.
It is encoded instead as a
.IR rune ,
in the private Unicode block that begins at
.B 0xF000
(see
.IR kbd (2)
and
.IR libkey ).
That rune's UTF-8 encoding is always three bytes, all of them above
.BR 0x7F ,
so no byte of the encoding can ever be mistaken for a character a
keyboard could otherwise type.
A left arrow, for instance, is
.BR U+F011 ,
and arrives at a reader of
.B /dev/cons
or a window's
.B cons
as those three bytes in order.
.I Kbd (2)
names every such key.
.PP
A key held with
.B Alt
is a
.IR chord :
it produces no character at all, held or not, because a chord is taken
by the window manager
.RI ( see
.IR intuition (4))
before any window sees it.
.SS The console's line
.B /dev/cons
holds one line under construction and cooks it: a typed character
appends to the line and echoes, a newline sends the line, and three
keys edit it:
.TP 8
.BR \eb " or " DEL
erase the last character
.TP
.B ^U
erase the whole line
.TP
.B ^D
send the line with no newline if it holds characters, or end the file
if it is empty
.PD
.PP
Any other control character is dropped and never reaches a reader.
.SS A window's line
A window
.RI ( see
.IR window (1))
takes its keyboard raw and cooks its own line, with the same rules
(see
.IR edit (2))
plus two more:
.TP 8
.B ^W
erase one word: the run of letters and digits before the cursor, and
any punctuation between them and it
.TP
.BR ^A ", " ^E
move the cursor to the beginning or the end of the line
.TP
left and right arrow
move the cursor one character
.PD
.PP
There is no
.B ^D
in a window's line: a window's
.B cons
never ends, and a partial line is never delivered without its newline.
.SS Modifiers
Shift, control, alt, caps lock, num lock and scroll lock are
.IR "modifier keys" :
they change the meaning of the keys held with them and produce no
character of their own.
A reader of a
.B kbd
file
(see
.IR kbdfs (4))
sees a modifier as a rune among the keys held, the same way it sees any
other key with no character.
.SS The kbd file
.IR Kbdfs (4)
serves a
.B kbd
file, one message per read:
.IP
.EX
c<runes>    the characters typed, as UTF-8
k<runes>    every key held down, after this change
K<runes>    every key held down, after this change
.EE
.PP
A
.B c
message carries what a press of a plain key would deliver to
.BR /dev/cons .
A
.B k
message is sent after a key goes down and a
.B K
message after one comes up, each carrying the rune every key held means
under the modifiers of the moment, one after another with no
separator.
A reader of
.B kbd
sees alt go down and a letter go down beside it, which is a chord, and
sees it come back up.
That is what lets a chord reach the window manager when it can never
reach
.BR /dev/cons .
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /dev/cons
the console, cooked
.TP
.B /n/kbd/cons
the characters typed, served by
.IR kbdfs (4)
.TP
.B /n/kbd/kbd
key events, served by
.IR kbdfs (4)
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR kbd (2),
.IR edit (2),
.IR kbdfs (4),
.IR intuition (4),
.IR window (1),
.IR keys (6)
.SH BUGS
Only a US layout exists.
There is no compose key and no way to type a rune outside the private
block by number.
